{"name":"Light Featherbed","hex":"#c1d8eb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"featherbed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mattress stuffed with feathers.","An area of bog where a layer of moss covers a pool of water or mud, presenting a hazard to walkers who may fall through it."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat someone with excessive indulgence; to pamper, cosset or mollycoddle.","To engage in featherbedding; to employ more workers than necessary to fulfill a labor union requirement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/featherbed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tick mattress","blocks":[{"text":"A tick mattress, bed tick or tick is a large bag made of strong, stiff, tightly-woven material (ticking). This is then filled to make a mattress, with material such as straw, chaff, horsehair, coarse wool or down feathers, and less commonly, leaves, grass, reeds, bracken, or seaweed. The whole stuffed mattress may also, more loosely, be called a tick. The tick mattress may then be sewn through to hold the filling in place, or the unsecured filling could be shaken and smoothed as the beds were aired each morning. A straw-filled bed tick is called a paillasse, palliasse, or pallet, and these terms may also be used for bed ticks with other fillings. A tick filled with flock is called a flockbed. A feather-filled tick is called a featherbed, and a down-filled one is a downbed; these can also be used above the sleeper as a duvet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Poston%2C_Arizona._Filling_straw_ticks_for_mattresses_at_Colorado_River_Relocation_center_for_evacuee_._._._-_NARA_-_536109.jpg/330px-Poston%2C_Arizona._Filling_straw_ticks_for_mattresses_at_Colorado_River_Relocation_center_for_evacuee_._._._-_NARA_-_536109.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tick_mattress","wikidata":"Q22907584"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg/500px-Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg/960px-Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg/500px-Wikimania_2014_-_Victoria_and_Albert_Museum_-_The_Great_Bed_of_Ware221398.jpg?utm_source=en.wikiped